  "x-status": "designed",
  "description": "Designed (docs/native/capture-split.md 4.3 \"Idempotency, on the hub\", 11 H1a to H1k, #773), not implemented. The hub's rules for `POST /api/station/urgent` as a table a test can run: each scenario is a list of steps and what the hub must answer and hold. tests/contracts_capture.py `Hub` is the reference model that passes it; the hub's route, and the stub hub the station's tests use, must give the same answers. A step has `at` (seconds on the HUB's clock since the scenario began; the hub's clock then reads `params.t0` + `at`) and one of: `urgent` (a request's items: each names an entry of `items`, with keys to replace; `data` is merged; `station` defaults to st-north and `sent_at` to the hub's clock) with `expect` (the status per item, in order; or `expect_http` for a request refused whole, with `reason`; `off_contract`: a body no conforming sender makes, which the hub must survive); `event` (one event of the app's lane, /api/station/events: its `start` and `end` are the STATION's wall clock, which need not be the hub's); `hello` (GET /api/station/hello; `gen_changed` says whether its `gen` is another than the last one answered); `ack`, `all_clear` (a person acknowledges, or clears, the alarm that hangs on this traffic id); `state_paging` (the `paging` list of the app's state post); `hub` (`handoff`: stop leading; `lead`: lead again, with a new generation; `{restore_s: n}`: crash and come back from a replica n seconds old); `holds` (what holds at that moment). `then` is what holds at the end, in the same words: `alarms`, `pushes`, `notices` (how many); `traffic_ids` (what each alarm hangs on); `push_age_s` (the first push's age); `still_set` (how often an alarm was marked \"emergency still set\"); `sources` (the stations of the first alarm); `states` (st-north's keys in the dedupe store: `raised`, `stale`, `seen`); `pages`, and `page` (the live page under its key: `rev`, `phase`, `ended`, `live`, `expires_in_s`). `params.max_age_s` is A7, NOT DECIDED by the admin: 1800 is the recommended rule, and a scenario that depends on it says so (`depends_on`). Every limit is exclusive on the inside (exactly the limit is outside); a slack written +- is inclusive.",
